  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component demonstrates strong foundational accessibility practices: native `<button>` elements are used for controls, focus indicators meet WCAG 2.4.13, new-tab links include proper `rel` attributes and visually hidden warnings, and the heading/landmark structure is logical. However, the map canvas acts as an inaccessible black hole for assistive technology, dynamic state changes from zoom actions are not communicated to AT, and the programmatic association between the map and its text alternative (the table link) is missing. These gaps prevent screen reader users from understanding the map's existence or tracking its state, requiring revision before shipping.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. Map canvas `<div>` lacks `role=\"img\"` and `aria-label`, rendering it invisible to screen readers as a distinct region.\n2. Dynamic updates from zoom/reset actions lack `aria-live` announcements, leaving screen reader users unaware of state changes.\n3. The \"text alternative\" table link is not programmatically associated with the map canvas via `aria-describedby`.\n4. Keyboard accessibility of the third-party map itself is unverified; external controls may not suffice for full map interaction (panning).\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Map Canvas Lacks Accessible Name and Role**\n   - Evidence: `<div className=\"map-canvas\" />` at `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:15` is an empty div with no ARIA attributes. Screen readers will announce nothing for this region, effectively hiding the map from AT users.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users.\n   - Expected behavior: Per WCAG 1.1.1 (Non-text Content) and WAI-ARIA `role=\"img\"` pattern, non-text content must have a text alternative or accessible name. A map region should be announced so users know what they are encountering before deciding to skip to the table alternative.\n   - Fix: Add `role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Watershed boundary map\"` to the `.map-canvas` div. Consider adding `aria-describedby=\"table-alt\"` to explicitly link it to the table alternative.\n\n2. **Dynamic Map State Changes Not Announced to Assistive Technology**\n   - Evidence: `handleZoomIn`, `handleZoomOut`, and `handleReset` at `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:4-6` only log to console. When these functions update the third-party map, no DOM state or live region updates to reflect the change.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users.\n   - Expected behavior: Per WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), dynamic content changes must be announced to assistive technology. Users need to know the zoom level changed or the view was reset.\n   - Fix: Implement an `aria-live=\"polite\"` region (e.g., `<div aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"sr-only\">Map {action}ed</div>`) that updates when zoom/reset actions complete, or update a status element inside the map container.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing Programmatic Association Between Map and Text Alternative**\n   - Evidence: The table link at `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:18` is a standard `<a>` tag with no ID, and the map div has no `aria-describedby`. The comment states it's the \"text alternative,\" but without ARIA association, AT users won't know the link belongs to the map.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users.\n   - Expected behavior: Per WCAG 1.1.1, when a text alternative is provided via a separate link, it must be programmatically associated with the non-text content using `aria-describedby`.\n   - Fix: Add `id=\"table-alt\"` to the table link and `aria-describedby=\"table-alt\"` to the `.map-canvas` div.\n\n2. **Unverified Keyboard Accessibility of Third-Party Map (Dependency Risk)**\n   - Evidence: The component mounts a third-party map library into `.map-canvas` but provides no mechanism to verify or enforce keyboard operability for the map itself. External controls only handle zoom/reset, not panning.\n   - User group impacted: Keyboard-only users.\n   - Expected behavior: Per WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), all functionality must be operable via keyboard. If the mounted map requires mouse-only panning or lacks focusable elements, keyboard users are locked out of the core content.\n   - Fix: Verify the third-party library supports full keyboard navigation (focus management, arrow key panning). If it does not, either swap to a keyboard-accessible map library or provide alternative pan controls within this component's control panel.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Button Touch Target Sizing**: `.map-control` uses `padding: 6px 14px`. Depending on inherited font size, the resulting hit area may fall below the 24x24 CSS pixel minimum recommended by WCAG 2.5.8. Verify computed dimensions; if <24px, increase padding or add `min-width`/`min-height`.\n- **Focus Indicator Contrast Verification**: The `3px solid #0b5fff` focus outline on white background likely passes WCAG 2.4.13 (3:1 contrast change), but should be measured against the exact computed background color of the button to confirm compliance.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Map Loading State**: If the third-party map takes time to render, consider adding `aria-busy=\"true\"` to the canvas during load and toggling it to `false` when ready, paired with a loading announcement.\n- **Explicit Map Type for AT**: Consider adding `aria-roledescription=\"map\"` to `.map-canvas` to provide additional context to screen readers about the region's purpose beyond just `role=\"img\"`.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Programmatic link between the map canvas and its text alternative table.\n- Live region or status update mechanism for dynamic zoom/reset actions.\n- Keyboard fallback or verification strategy for the third-party map dependency.\n- `aria-live` or `aria-busy` handling for map initialization/loading states.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Watershed Boundary Map\" heading, then encounters an empty div (or unlabelled canvas) with no indication of what it contains or that a table alternative exists. Clicking zoom buttons produces no auditory feedback. The experience is fragmented and state-blind.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Can tab through the three buttons logically. However, if the mounted map does not support keyboard panning/zooming natively, the user cannot interact with the core content at all. External controls only change zoom, leaving pan inaccessible without mouse.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**: Layout reflows correctly within `max-width: 640px`. Focus indicators are visible and meet contrast thresholds. Map background (`#eaecf0`) provides clear separation. No horizontal scroll expected.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Button labels (\"Zoom in\", \"Zoom out\", \"Reset view\") are explicit and unambiguous. The new-tab link includes a clear visual and screen-reader-only warning. Interaction model is consistent and predictable.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component passes basic semantic structure checks but fails on two critical accessibility design decisions: (1) the map canvas is invisible to assistive technology due to missing `role`/`aria-label`, and (2) dynamic state changes are silent, breaking the feedback loop for non-visual users. Additionally, the text alternative link lacks programmatic association, and keyboard access to the map itself is an unmitigated dependency risk. These are not preference issues; they block core functionality understanding and operability for screen reader and keyboard users. Revision is required to add ARIA associations, live region announcements, and verify third-party keyboard support.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the third-party map library natively support keyboard navigation and focus management? If not, what is the mitigation strategy?\n- Is the table linked below truly a *complete* equivalent of the map's spatial data, or does it only cover boundary coordinates? Complex maps often require geographic descriptions beyond tabular data.\n- Does the `aria-live` region need to be `assertive` if zoom actions interrupt user reading flow, or is `polite` sufficient for status updates?",
